# testing — what a test needs that the LANGUAGE does not give it.
#
# Bundled into a program that writes `import "testing"`; its members are reached through
# the namespace, as `testing.assert_raises(…)`. This is the library the `#[test]` discovery
# + `zerg test` runner builds on.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS NOT HERE ANY MORE, and its absence is the module's shape. `assert cond`
# is a keyword (GRAMMAR#assert-stmt): the compiler writes the failure message, and it can
# say the three things a function never could — the file and line the claim was written at,
# the claim's own source text, and the value of each operand a comparison came apart into.
# A function cannot know any of them. Zerg has no `__FILE__` and no caller attribution, and
# a condition arrives here as a `bool` with its shape already compiled away; `assert_eq`
# bought back two of those values by taking the operands apart at the CALL, which is why it
# existed and why it no longer has to.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT IS WHAT A KEYWORD CANNOT DO. `assert_raises` takes the outcome of a `guard`
# and hands back the `Err`, which is a value-level question about a call that already
# happened. The Context is the channel a running test says things over. Neither is an
# assertion, and nothing in this module raises a message a reader parses: a claim that did
# not hold is `AssertionError`, raised by the keyword, and the runner tells that from a
# crash by the KIND rather than by the text.

import (
	"log"
	"strings"
)

# not_raised is what assert_raises does when the call it was handed came back. It is a
# function rather than a `raise` written into the match arm because both arms of a match
# answer one type, and this one has to answer an `Err` it never gets to build.
fn not_raised() -> Err {
	raise "assert_raises failed: the call returned instead of raising"
}

# assert_raises answers the `Err` a call raised; it FAILS when the call returned instead.
# What it takes is the `guard` the caller writes at the point of the call:
#
#   e := testing.assert_raises(guard { strings.split("a,b", "") })
#   assert e is ValueError
#
# THE ERROR COMES BACK RATHER THAN THE KIND GOING IN, and that is not a preference: a type
# is not a value in `zerg`, so `assert_raises(f, ValueError)` cannot be spelled at all. The
# form that is left is also the better half of the trade — the caller asks with the
# language's own `is`, and this module never has to learn the error taxonomy it is
# deliberately outside of (see the header).
#
# A `guard` AND NOT AN `fn ()`, and the difference was measured rather than preferred. The
# closure reads better — `assert_raises(fn () { strings.split("a,b", "") })` says what it
# does — and this compiler cannot compile it: a closure body naming an imported module is
# _E735 a closure captures `strings`, and this compiler cannot work out what it holds_,
# because a namespace is a free name and a capture needs a type. Every test that reaches
# its module through `import` is exactly that shape, so the closure form would be a form
# that works only for the tests that need it least. The `guard` is evaluated at the CALL
# SITE, where the namespace is in scope, and hands the outcome over as an ordinary value.
#
# IT IS GENERIC BECAUSE THE CARRIER IS — `split` raises out of a `list[str]`, `repeat` out
# of a `str`, `count` out of an `int` — which is what a suite that hand-rolled this helper
# found and had to write for itself. `T` is never inspected here; the whole body is about
# the Right.
pub fn assert_raises[T](r: Result[T]) -> Err {
	return match r {
		Either.Left(_)  => not_raised()
		Either.Right(e) => e
	}
}

# --- the test context ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE CHAIN IS GONE, AND THE ASSERTION IS WHAT TOOK IT. `t.str("file", path).assert(cond,
# "…")` existed for one reason: an assertion that said only "assertion failed" needed
# somewhere to hang the facts that would make it readable, and the chain was that place.
# The keyword now reports the position, the claim's own text and the operand values, which
# is most of what a chain was ever loaded with — and its terminal is FORCED out anyway,
# since `assert` is a keyword and cannot name a method.
#
# What a chain was genuinely for beyond that is a DOMAIN NOTE, a thing about the fixture
# rather than about the expression: which row, which fixture file, which platform. `log` is
# already that and is better at it — it is printed only when the test fails, it is not
# attached to one assertion, and it needs no builder to be finished. So a suite that used
# to write the chain writes `ctx.log("row 42")` above its asserts and loses nothing.
#
# A Context is taken BY VALUE — `fn my_test(ctx: testing.Context)` — and by value is not
# a copy of what matters. The one field that carries anything out is a channel, and a
# channel is a `Ref` value: copying a value refcount-bumps the `Ref`s it contains and
# deep-copies the rest (docs/code/coroutine.md). So the copy the parameter makes shares
# the one thing that must be shared, and a test says what it has to say without `mut &`
# — measured: a Context passed by value through two frames and a `spawn` delivered every
# event to the runner holding the receiving end.
#
# WHAT A TEST SAYS TRAVELS AS AN EVENT, not as a field it sets, and that is the same
# reasoning one step on: a `bool` field set by the test would be set on the COPY, and the
# runner would never see it. The channel is the only field, so it is the only mechanism.

# Event is one thing a running test told its runner. It is `pub` because the generated
# driver names the channel's type; its fields are the module's own, because nothing
# outside reads one — `collect` below is the only reader there is.
pub struct Event {
	kind: int = 0
	text: str = ""
}

# The kinds an Event carries. LOG / SKIP / FATAL are what the test itself says; DONE, RAISE
# and CRASH are what the driver says about the body afterwards, over the same channel, so
# that one receive loop sees the whole of a test's life and there is no second route for
# a runner to get wrong.
#
# RAISE AND CRASH ARE THE SAME EVENT ASKED A DIFFERENT QUESTION, and the question is the
# error's KIND. A claim that did not hold is `AssertionError` and is a FAILED TEST; anything
# else that reached the top of the body is a program that fell over, and calling that a
# failed assertion sends the reader looking for an assertion that does not exist. The driver
# asks with the language's own `is`, which is why this needs a kind rather than a message.
const EV_LOG := 0
const EV_SKIP := 1
const EV_FATAL := 2
const EV_DONE := 3
const EV_RAISE := 4
const EV_CRASH := 5

# Outcome is what one test came to, as the runner reports it — `ok`, `fail`, `skip` or
# `crash`, which is the set `zergc.zg`'s report format lists — and the text that goes under
# it: a skip's reason, or a failure's notes and message. (`timeout` is the driver's own and
# never comes from here: a test that never finished said nothing to collect.)
pub struct Outcome {
	pub verdict: str = ""
	pub text: str = ""
}

pub struct Context {
	# `name` is the method, so the field cannot be
	tname: str = ""

	# THE CHANNEL IS BIDIRECTIONAL, and the direction is what makes the runner's loop
	# end: a channel closes when its last SEND-capable holder goes, and the runner holds
	# a `<-chan[Event]` which is not one. So the context — held only by the coroutine
	# running the test — is the last sender, and the body returning is what ends the
	# stream.
	#
	# THE FIELD IS `pub` BECAUSE THE ALTERNATIVE IS A DEFAULT NOBODY CAN WRITE. A
	# module-private field must carry one (`E482`), and the only value that would serve is
	# a FRESH channel per construction — `chan[Event]()`, a call. `zerg` takes that: a
	# default is evaluated per construction and may be a call (docs/core/types.md, "Field
	# defaults"). The bootstrap SEED does not, and `testing` is a module the seed's own
	# suite compiles, so the module has to stay inside the subset the seed reads. `pub`
	# is what drops the requirement instead of working around it: `context` below hands
	# every Context its channel, so the default was dead the day it was written.
	pub events: chan[Event]
}

# context builds the Context for one test. It is the generated driver's entry point and
# not something a test writes: a test is HANDED one.
pub fn context(name: str, events: chan[Event]) -> Context {
	return Context(name, events)
}

impl Context {
	# name is which test this is — the function's own name, as the report prints it.
	pub fn name() -> str {
		return this.tname
	}

	# log records a message to show ONLY IF this test fails. A test that logs its way
	# through a passing run is a test whose output nobody reads; the note earns its place at
	# the moment there is something to explain.
	#
	# IT IS THE LOGGER SAYING IT. The message goes through this context's own `log.Logger`, so
	# a note carries the same timestamp and the same level marker every other line in this
	# tree does, and there is one renderer rather than two. The rendered line's trailing
	# newline comes off because the runner joins notes with its own.
	#
	# `log` IS BOTH A METHOD HERE AND AN IMPORTED MODULE, and that is not a collision: `E705`
	# keys a name by its receiver, so `Context.log` and the namespace `log` never meet. It was
	# checked before the module was written.
	pub fn log(msg: str) {
		this.events <- Event(EV_LOG, rendered(msg))
	}

	# skip says this test does not apply here — a platform it is not for, a resource that
	# is not present — and stops it. IT IS NOT A FAILURE, and the report counts it apart
	# from both.
	#
	# THE UNWIND SAYS "STOPPED" AND THE CONTEXT SAYS "WHY", which is the whole reason
	# there is no error KIND for either of these. `testing` is deliberately outside the
	# taxonomy (see the header): a claim that did not hold is not a value that was
	# refused, and a runner that told a skip from a failure by matching on a message
	# string would be reading prose as a protocol. So the raise carries nothing anyone
	# reads, the event carries everything, and the runner asks the context.
	pub fn skip(reason: str) -> Result[nil] {
		this.events <- Event(EV_SKIP, reason)
		raise "test skipped"
	}

	# fatal stops this test now, failed. It is what a test says when going on would only
	# produce noise — a fixture that did not open, a parse that returned nothing to
	# assert about.
	pub fn fatal(msg: str) -> Result[nil] {
		this.events <- Event(EV_FATAL, msg)
		raise "test failed"
	}
}

# rendered is `msg` as a LOG LINE — the same line `log` writes everywhere else in this tree,
# with the same timestamp and the same level marker, rather than a second idea of what a line
# looks like. Two unconnected notions of "a log line" is the drift that put the JSON escaper in
# one place, and this is that argument applied to the other one.
#
# THE LOGGER IS BUILT HERE AND NOT HELD ON THE CONTEXT, and that was measured rather than
# preferred: a `log.Logger` stored in the Context and reached through `this` deadlocked — the
# line went into the sink's channel and the receive on the Context's copy of it never saw it.
# A Context is copied by value at every frame and every `spawn`, and this module's own header
# explains that a copy shares its `Ref`s and deep-copies the rest; something in that path does
# not hold for a channel two structs down. Built locally it is one channel, one send, one
# receive, in one frame — and the cost is one logger per NOTE, which is a thing a test writes
# when it has something to explain rather than in a loop.
#
# PRETTY, UNCOLOURED, AND AT A LEVEL THIS LINE IS ON, WHATEVER THE ENVIRONMENT SAYS. A note is
# read by a person looking at a failure report, and the report's shape belongs to the runner
# rather than to `ZERG_LOG` — a suite whose notes turned into JSON because a shell exported a
# variable would be a suite that reads differently depending on who ran it. The trailing
# newline comes off because the runner joins notes with its own.
#
# THE LEVEL IS THE ONE OF THE THREE THAT HANGS RATHER THAN LOOKS WRONG, and it was measured:
# `log.new()` reads `ZERG_LOG_LEVEL`, so under `ZERG_LOG_LEVEL=warn` the `.info()` below is a
# DEAD entry, `msg` writes nothing, and the receive on a one-slot channel whose only sender is
# this same frame parks forever. Every `ctx.log(…)` in every suite in the tree would hang the
# runner, for a variable that has nothing to do with tests. `TRACE` and not `INFO`, so that
# there is no level at all at which a note is silently dropped.
fn rendered(msg: str) -> str {
	lines := chan[str](1)
	log.new().level(log.Level.TRACE).format(log.Format.PRETTY).colour(false).to(log.to_chan(lines)).info().msg(msg)

	# THE RESULT IS ASSIGNED RATHER THAN RETURNED FROM INSIDE THE `if`, and that is not a style
	# choice: `zerg fmt` collapses a lone `return` in an `if` into the postfix form, and
	# `return … if line := <-lines` DOES NOT PARSE — the trailing `if` on a diverge takes a bare
	# CONDITION and a binding head is not one (`E205`). An assignment is not a diverge, so the
	# formatter leaves this shape alone. Reported; four lines reproduce it.
	mut out := msg
	if line := <-lines {
		out = strings.trim_suffix(line, "\n")
	}
	return out
}

# finished is what the driver says after the body is out of its hands: `ok` when it
# returned, and otherwise the message it raised. It is the last event on the channel.
#
# `claim` is whether what came out was an `AssertionError` — a claim the test made that did
# not hold — as against any other Err, which is the program falling over. The driver asks it
# with `e is AssertionError` at the point it holds the Err, because that is the only place
# the Err exists: by here there is a message, and telling the two apart from a message is
# reading prose as a protocol.
pub fn finished(ctx: Context, ok: bool, msg: str, claim: bool) {
	if ok {
		ctx.events <- Event(EV_DONE, "")
	} else if claim {
		ctx.events <- Event(EV_RAISE, msg)
	} else {
		ctx.events <- Event(EV_CRASH, msg)
	}
}

# collect drains one test's channel and says what the test came to.
#
# A MISSING TERMINAL EVENT IS A CRASH, and it is the default rather than a branch: the
# loop ends either because the sender finished — which it does only after `finished` —
# or because the coroutine died, and only the first can have said DONE, RAISE or CRASH. A death
# also raises out of the receive (the channel closes carrying the producer's Err, or the
# runtime finds nothing left to run), which is what the `guard` is for; both routes land
# on the same verdict.
pub fn collect(rx: <-chan[Event]) -> Outcome {
	mut notes := ""
	mut verdict := "crash"
	mut text := ""
	mut why := ""
	mut skipped := false

	guard {
		for ev in rx {
			if ev.kind == EV_LOG {
				notes = notes + ev.text + "\n"
			} else if ev.kind == EV_SKIP {
				skipped = true
				why = ev.text
			} else if ev.kind == EV_FATAL {
				why = ev.text
			} else if ev.kind == EV_DONE {
				verdict = "ok"
			} else if ev.kind == EV_RAISE {
				verdict = "fail"
				text = ev.text
			} else if ev.kind == EV_CRASH {
				verdict = "crash"
				text = ev.text
			}
		}
	}

	return Outcome("ok", text) if verdict == "ok"

	# `skip` and `fatal` both arrive as a raise that is NOT a claim, so both land on CRASH —
	# and the context is what tells them from each other and from a program that really did
	# fall over. Neither is a crash, and `fatal` is a failure the test itself declared, so each
	# takes its own verdict back here.
	return Outcome("skip", why) if skipped
	return Outcome("fail", notes + why) if why != ""

	return Outcome(verdict, notes + text)
}
